# Copy to .env.local. That file is gitignored.

# ─── Writing ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Required for POST /v1/cards and GET /v1/fields. One shared passcode: one
# person edits this collection, so an account system with a password reset
# behind it would be furniture. Any long random string; `openssl rand -hex 24`.
#
# Without it every write answers 503 and says so, which is the intended state
# for a deployment nobody adds cards from.
# The address that signs in, beside CARDS_TOKEN. One account, so this is a
# single value rather than a table; it is also what the login field is filled
# in with, so it is not a secret. Without it nobody can sign in and the form
# says so rather than refusing every password.
OWNER_EMAIL=

CARDS_TOKEN=

# ─── Who may post ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Comma separated. Browsers on *other* origins may write from here; anything
# else not listed is 403. A request with no Origin header at all is not a
# browser (the iOS app, curl) and is allowed: neither can be tricked into
# posting on someone else's behalf, which is the risk this defends against.
#
# This app's own pages do not need to be listed here, on any domain: guard.ts's
# sameOrigin() compares Origin against the request's own host and allows it
# regardless of this list. Leaving it empty only blocks a *different* site or
# tool from posting here — never this one.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000

# ─── Where this deploys ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The app's own absolute address, used for canonicals, og:url, the sitemap and
# robots.txt. Optional in production: without it, SITE_URL in lib/core/config.ts
# falls back to Vercel's own VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL, then to localhost.
# Set explicitly whenever a custom domain is in front of Vercel, so a canonical
# never points at a *.vercel.app address instead of the real one.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ─── Whose collection ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing to set. PUBLIC_USERNAME and OWNER_NAME were here and are gone: whose a
# collection is, and what to call them, come from the profile being rendered
# (lib/core/owner.ts) rather than from the deployment's environment.

# ─── The database ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Required. Both of these are public by design: the URL is in every request
# the browser makes and the anon key is in the JavaScript bundle. What stops a
# stranger using them is row level security, not secrecy — see
# supabase/migrations/20260814062300_accounts_and_cards.sql, where the policies are.
# If RLS were off, these two lines would be the whole database.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# This one is not public and never goes near the browser. It bypasses every
# policy above, which is exactly why only the account-deletion path may hold
# it. Supabase: Project Settings → API keys.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ─── How much of a set to price ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional. The catalogue is cached per set and shared by everyone who owns a
# card from it, so pricing a whole set once can be cheaper than pricing each
# owner's cards separately — but only once a set has more than one owner. Zero
# means "only the cards somebody actually holds", which is what this did before
# any of this existed and is the right setting until there is a second account.
# Set it to 400 when there is.
CATALOGUE_SET_PRICING_MAX=0

# --- The screenshot harness ------------------------------------------------
# The account `visual/auth.setup.ts` signs in as, so `npm run visual` can
# photograph the signed-in screens — /collection, /dashboard, /settings,
# /wishlist and the add-card dialog. Without it the owner project cannot run,
# and ADR-0020 is a regression that hid behind exactly that gap for weeks.
#
# It must be an account that has confirmed its email and signed in at least
# once. OWNER_EMAIL is not it: that variable is a leftover ADR-0034 took out of
# the code, and its account was never confirmed — generate_link succeeds, the
# app's /auth/confirm bounces to /login, and the failure says only "expected
# /dashboard".
#
# To find the right one with the service-role key:
#   GET  {SUPABASE_URL}/rest/v1/cards?select=user_id      -> whose collection is biggest
#   GET  {SUPABASE_URL}/auth/v1/admin/users/{that_id}     -> its email
VISUAL_EMAIL=you@example.com
